frontends/nss: accelerate the tag_8021q ports of a VLAN-aware bridge

A tag_8021q DSA port of a VLAN-unaware bridge is, on the wire, a VLAN upper
of its conduit with the bridge's tag_8021q VID, and 0051 makes it that in
the interface hierarchy: a VLAN iface above the conduit's ethernet leaf, so
the rule ends on the conduit and carries the VID. A port of a VLAN-aware
bridge is not that: its frames cross the conduit with the bridge's own VIDs
(the CPU port is a tagged member of every bridge VLAN), and the port
carries as many of them as it is a member of. Until now 0051 still gave it
the tag_8021q bridge VID: the rule was written with a VID the flow never
uses, the firmware matched almost nothing, the flow ran on the host at
62-83 % softirq with 90-210 Mbit/s and retransmissions (measured on an
Archer AX55 v1, wired VLAN 10 to WAN), and the few frames it did emit went
out in the tag_8021q VLAN, which the switch delivers to the bridge's ports
regardless of their VLAN.

Now such a port is no VLAN iface at all: a plain ethernet leaf with no
interface number - also when the glue bound one of the bridge's VLAN nodes
to it, since that node names one VLAN and the flow may be in another - which
every frontend refuses: the non-ported and multicast ones keep those flows
on the host path. The ported frontends
resolve the flow themselves: the VID is the one ECM recorded for the port
in the connection's bridge VLAN filter data, taken only when the flow and
the return direction agree on it (they can differ for a host with one MAC
in several VLANs, or a routed flow through the bridge master to a trunk
port); the endpoint is the conduit's interface; and the qca-dsa-nss glue
must have a firmware VLAN node of that VID on the conduit, since a tagged
frame with no node goes to the host unparsed. Without an agreed VID or a
node the flow is refused rather than tagged wrong. The VID is that side's
one tag, whether the port is a tagged or an untagged member - the conduit
carries the tag either way - so a VLAN device of the bridge above such a
port (br-lan.10 over lan1) keeps its own tag out of the rule; the VAP case
of 0038 is unchanged.

The lookup into the glue is resolved with symbol_get() at first use, so
the ECM module neither links against the glue nor needs it loaded.

